Well, I already responded on g+ but to go briefly again: what they talk abo=
ut has little to do with Whorf at least (it's harder to say about Sapir), w=
ho explicitly rejects relationships to vocabulary and to particular cultura=
l items, referring always to grammatical categories and habitual thought (n=
a=EFve metaphysics).=A0 But what they do talk about is what all too many pe=
ople mean by SWH, often including JCB and subsequent loglangists.=A0 They q=
uite rightly (I think) ridicule much of this, but fail to note that there i=
s more (or different) to SWH that is not affected by the problems of the ve=
rsions they discuss.=A0 Incidentally, real SWH is pretty irrelevant to logl=
angs, Loglan and Lojban spectacularly so.




On Sunday, August 4, 2013 12:17 PM, la gusek. <gunnar_secret@hotmail.com> w=
rote:
 =20
Yeah, I felt that was quite biased. I mean, sure, it's a controversial topi=
c and there isn't much support for strong Sapir-Whorfianism today. But, the=
y do acknowledge certain different thinking patterns (like the way you memo=
rize an describe experiences or knowing which way is north), but they are v=
ery clear with that everything related to Sapir-Whorf is stupid and ridicul=
ous. It seems more of a bashing than a discussion, but that's my five cents=
. I don't believe in Sapir-Whorf as originally hypothesized though.

Also, Lojban is (of course) mentioned. But they are not discussing it very =
positive, and somewhat thinking that it's an impossible and ridiculous lang=
uage to try and learn. Like that they're not seriously believing it could b=
e fun or useful in real life, but a cute attempt of nerds over-believing Sa=
pir-Whorf (and trying to gain "magical abilities"). Laadan seems to be pref=
erred, for some reason.

Yeah, that's what I got from it! Worth a listening, but I do not fully agre=
e with them. Seems too much like a contra-cultural reaction to people belie=
ving Sapir-Whorf to a extreme level and irritating people with the classic =
Eskimo-snow-myth.
